## Experience and Lessons
|Case|:**active_web_server**
   - **Error Analysis:** All tests failed due to the inability of the Selenium webdriver to connect to the application running on `localhost:5000`. The error message indicated `net::ERR_CONNECTION_REFUSED`, indicating that there was no active server to handle the requests.
   - **Guidance:** Implement a web server framework such as Flask that enables routing for web pages and handles incoming requests and responses effectively. This will form the backbone of your web application.
|Case|:**server_setup**
   - **Error Analysis:** The application was not set up to run as a web server from `main.py`, leading to connectivity issues when tests tried to access endpoints like `/`, `/login`, and `/register`.
   - **Guidance:** Define properly routed endpoints to ensure all endpoints referenced in unit tests match those implemented in your Flask application. Ensure handlers are in place for each route (e.g., `/register`, `/bookmarks/`, etc.).

